𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟴/𝟯𝟬 – 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗣𝗲𝗿𝗳𝗼𝗿𝗺𝗮𝗻𝗰𝗲 𝗢𝗽𝘁𝗶𝗺𝗶𝘇𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻

Continuing my #30DaysOfAnsible journey! 🔥

Today I focused on Ansible Performance Optimization — because when infrastructure grows, automation speed matters.

⚡ 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗣𝗲𝗿𝗳𝗼𝗿𝗺𝗮𝗻𝗰𝗲 𝗠𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘀

Running Ansible against 5 servers is very different from running it against 500 or 5,000 servers.

Poorly optimized playbooks can lead to:

❌ Longer deployment times
❌ Higher resource usage
❌ Unnecessary network communication
❌ Slower CI/CD pipelines

Here are some techniques I learned:

🚀 𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁 𝗚𝗮𝘁𝗵𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗻𝗴

If facts aren't required, disable them:

- hosts: web
  gather_facts: false

This can reduce unnecessary work at the beginning of a play.

⚡ 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗿𝗲𝗻𝗰𝘆

Ansible can execute tasks across multiple hosts in parallel.

Control the number of simultaneous hosts using:

- hosts: web
  serial: 10

This is also useful for rolling deployments, where you don't want to update every server at once.

🔄 𝗔𝘃𝗼𝗶𝗱 𝗨𝗻𝗻𝗲𝗰𝗲𝘀𝘀𝗮𝗿𝘆 𝗧𝗮𝘀𝗸𝘀

Use Ansible modules instead of repeatedly executing shell commands where possible.

For example:

- name: Install Nginx
  ansible.builtin.apt:
    name: nginx
    state: present

Ansible can determine whether a change is actually required.

📦 𝗥𝗲𝘂𝘀𝗲 𝗩𝗮𝗿𝗶𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀 & 𝗥𝗼𝗹𝗲𝘀

Well-structured roles and variables make playbooks easier to maintain and reduce duplicated logic.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Performance optimization isn't just about making Ansible faster.

It's about designing automation that remains efficient, predictable, and scalable as infrastructure grows. 🚀
